  "affiliation_unit": "Shenyang Technical Reconnaissance Bureau",
  "affiliation_detail": "2022 Testimony before the U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission Hearing on \"China’s Cyber Capabilities: Warfare, Espionage, and Implications for the United States\" suspects Tonto Team's ties to the PLA's Shenyang-based Technical Reconnaissance Bureau (Unit 65016). (USCC, 2022, p.95)\nFireEye's 2017 reporting described Tonto Team as \"tied to China's military and based out of the northeastern Chinese city of Shenyang.\" (WSJ, 2017) Recorded Future's TAG-74 research assessed the group's targeting aligns with \"the likely operational purview of the Northern Theater Command,\" whose area of responsibility covers the Korean Peninsula, Japan, Mongolia, and Russia's Far East. (Recorded Future, 2023)\nNo individuals have been indicted.\n",
  "affiliation_confidence": "likely",
  "entity_name": null,
  "last_seen": "2023",
  "first_seen": "2009",
  "first_described": "2013-01",
  "description": "Tonto Team is a Chinese state-sponsored cyber espionage group active since at least 2009, assessed to operate under the PLA within the Northern Theater Command. The group has targeted government, military, diplomatic, energy, mining, financial, education, healthcare, and technology organizations, initially focusing on South Korea, Japan, Taiwan, and the United States before expanding into Eastern Europe and Russia around 2020. (MITRE, G0131)\nThe group's earliest documented activity is the Heartbeat Campaign (2009-2012), identified by Trend Micro, which targeted South Korean political parties, government agencies, military branches, and media. (Cisco Talos, 2020) In 2017, FireEye attributed attacks on South Korea's  THAAD missile defense program to Tonto Team. (WSJ, 2017). In 2020, Kaspersky  observed the group targeting financial and military organizations in Eastern Europe. (Kaspersky, 2020)\nFollowing Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Tonto Team was observed increasing espionage operations against Russian government agencies and scientific institutions, delivering Bisonal via Royal Road RTF exploits disguised as security advisories. SentinelOne noted a potential \"increase in intelligence collection requirements from inside Russia.\" (SentinelOne via Dark Reading, 2022; Malwarebytes, 2022) \nThe group also targeted cybersecurity firm Group-IB in 2021 and 2022, deploying the Bisonal.DoubleT variant and a previously undocumented downloader dubbed QuickMute. (Group-IB, 2023) In 2023, AhnLab reported Tonto Team targeting Korean education, construction, diplomatic, and political institutions,  \"using a file related to anti-malware products to ultimately execute their malicious attacks.\" (AhnLab, 2023)\n",
  "attack_group_id": "G0131",
  "attack_group_url": "https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0131/",
